---
import BaseLayout from "@/layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Privacy"
  description="What this site keeps when you visit or send me a message, and how to get it deleted."
>
  <section class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-4 py-10 sm:px-6">
    <header class="mb-6">
      <h1 class="text-2xl font-light tracking-tight sm:text-[30px]">Privacy</h1>
    </header>

    <div class="copy">
      <p>
        This site has no cookies and no ads of its own. Here's what it does
        keep, and what I do with it.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-xl font-light tracking-tight">Messages</h2>
      <p>
        When you send me a message, the form passes along your name, email
        address and message, plus the time you sent it. I only use them to
        reply, and to arrange a print if it comes to that. Nobody else gets
        them, and you won't end up on a mailing list.
      </p>
      <p>
        Messages are kept in my own Microsoft Azure account, on servers in the
        US, and a copy is emailed to my inbox through Azure so I see it sooner.
        They stay until I delete them. Ask through the form if you'd like yours
        gone and I'll take care of it.
      </p>
      <p>
        The form also counts how many messages come from one connection each
        hour, to keep spam down. It does that with a scrambled code made from
        your IP address, so the address itself is never saved.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-xl font-light tracking-tight">Visit counts</h2>
      <p>
        Each page you open is noted, along with how long you stayed on it. If
        another site sent you here, its name is saved too (google.com, say,
        never the full link).
      </p>
      <p>
        Visitors are counted without the site knowing who they are. It mixes
        your IP address and browser with the date and a secret key, then keeps
        only the scrambled result. That code changes every day, so visits on
        different days can't be linked. Your IP address is never stored.
      </p>
      <p>
        It also looks up who runs the network you're on and roughly where it is.
        That's usually your internet provider and a nearby city, or a company's
        name if you're on its own network. The lookup uses a copy of DB-IP's
        free data kept on the site's own servers, so your IP address isn't sent
        anywhere and still isn't saved. Whether you're on a phone or a computer,
        and which browser, is noted too.
      </p>
      <p>
        Each page also reports how fast it loaded for you, any script errors it
        ran into, whether you're in light or dark mode and roughly how wide your
        screen is. That's how I find a slow or broken page. On photo pages it
        counts which photos you open full size and for how long, and on the
        hobby pages whether you had a go and how your Narrow nature rounds went.
      </p>
      <p>
        On the weekdays side of the front page, where my resume is, the site
        notes how far you scroll, how long each part stays on screen and which
        links you press. If you copy some of my text, the first hundred
        characters are saved too. I use all this to see whether my resume and
        this site are actually doing their job.
      </p>
      <p>
        Links in my resume end in ?via=resume so I can tell those visits apart.
        The page takes it out of the address bar as soon as it loads. Opening
        the resume PDF is counted the same way, with the same network lookup.
        When someone reads the resume side for a while, I get an email with the
        network and city, the device, operating system and browser, where the
        visit came from and how long it was read. It also says how many links
        were pressed and how many times text was copied. It never says which
        links or what text.
      </p>
      <p>
        If you paste a link to this site into an app like Slack, Teams or
        LinkedIn, the app fetches a preview picture, and the site notes which
        app it was and which page. That request comes from the app's servers, so
        nothing about you is in it.
      </p>
      <p>
        Network lookups: <a
          class="draw"
          href="https://db-ip.com"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener noreferrer">IP Geolocation by DB-IP</a
        >.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-xl font-light tracking-tight">On your device</h2>
      <p>
        Your light or dark setting is saved in your browser, and so is your
        progress on the hobby pages, like the tide pool critters you've found.
        None of it leaves your device, and clearing this site's data in your
        browser wipes it.
      </p>
      <p>
        While a tab is open, it also holds a random code that matches each page
        view with how long it lasted. Closing the tab clears it.
      </p>
      <p>
        The selfie to bird generator reads your photo right on your device. It's
        never uploaded.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-xl font-light tracking-tight">Other sites</h2>
      <p>
        The site runs on Microsoft Azure, and their servers handle every
        request, like any web host.
      </p>
      <p>
        Some hobby pages load photos and sightings straight from iNaturalist.
        When they do, your browser connects to iNaturalist, which sees your IP
        address like any site you visit.
      </p>
      <p>
        The aquarium page plays a video of my tank from YouTube once you scroll
        down to it. It uses YouTube's privacy-enhanced player, so watching it
        there doesn't shape what YouTube recommends to you or the ads you see.
        YouTube still sees your IP address and can keep a little data in your
        browser, like any page with a YouTube video.
      </p>
      <p>
        The weekdays side of the front page shows the planes flying over Seattle
        right now, from seattlebalc.com. Your browser asks that site for them
        while the panel is on screen, so it sees your IP address too. One of my
        YouTube videos sits next to it and only loads from YouTube's
        privacy-enhanced player when you press Play.
      </p>
      <p>
        Nothing here follows you around other sites. Visit counts work the same
        whether or not your browser sends Do Not Track. I don't sell or pass on
        anything about you.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-xl font-light tracking-tight">Your data</h2>
      <p>
        Send me a message if you want to know what I have from you, or want it
        fixed or deleted, and I'll sort it out. If any of this changes, I'll
        update this page and the date below.
      </p>
      <button
        type="button"
        class="linkbtn draw"
        data-contact-open
        data-contact-message="I have a question about my data."
      >
        <span>Ask about your data</span>
      </button>
    </div>

    <p class="u-label mt-10">
      Last updated <time datetime="2026-11-12">November 12, 2026</time>
    </p>

    <p class="mt-12">
      <a class="u-label" href="/photographs/">← All sessions</a>
    </p>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .copy {
    max-width: 65ch;
  }
  .copy p {
    margin: 0 0 14px;
    color: var(--ink-3);
    font-size: 15px;
    line-height: 1.6;
  }
  .copy h2 {
    margin: 44px 0 12px;
  }
</style>
